WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.043 --> 00:00:04.629
(creepy music)

00:00:12.762 --> 00:00:15.348
- [Narrator] The internet,

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:18.601
what began as exclusive
tool to connect militaries,

00:00:18.601 --> 00:00:21.271
governments, and then universities,

00:00:21.271 --> 00:00:23.815
has now, more than 60 years later,

00:00:23.815 --> 00:00:26.234
transformed everything about how

00:00:26.234 --> 00:00:29.654
modern societies operate and communicate.

00:00:30.613 --> 00:00:34.701
Today, no one can escape
the grasp of social media,

00:00:34.701 --> 00:00:37.412
or evade the mind-boggling connectivity

00:00:37.412 --> 00:00:41.583
that nearly all humans
worldwide, now have access too.

00:00:45.462 --> 00:00:48.882
But few have been prepared
for what has also emerged,

00:00:48.882 --> 00:00:51.801
with all this human connectedness.

00:00:51.801 --> 00:00:55.263
A new, and ominous,
strain of human behavior

00:00:55.263 --> 00:00:56.848
has been unleashed.

00:00:59.267 --> 00:01:00.268
Increasingly,

00:01:00.268 --> 00:01:03.438
people are hiding behind
a cloak of anonymity,

00:01:03.438 --> 00:01:07.734
waiting to provoke, harass,
and even threaten anyone

00:01:07.734 --> 00:01:09.235
they choose.

00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:12.155
They are called, Internet Trolls.

00:01:12.155 --> 00:01:14.324
(sinister laugh)

00:01:14.324 --> 00:01:18.161
- A troll is someone, devoting
larger parts of their life,

00:01:18.161 --> 00:01:21.581
to attacking other people on the internet.

00:01:21.581 --> 00:01:23.500
Basically, trying to make
that other person's life

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:25.335
miserable in some way.

00:01:26.211 --> 00:01:27.670
- They sent things to my home.

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:29.672
It terrifies me to think
what they could do,

00:01:29.672 --> 00:01:32.550
if they wanted to, knowing
exactly where I lived,

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.761
knowing where my children go to school.

00:01:34.761 --> 00:01:37.180
(bell rings)

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:39.307
- [Narrator] Trolls can
be a minor annoyance,

00:01:39.307 --> 00:01:41.559
or a life crippling terror.

00:01:42.769 --> 00:01:46.439
But what happens, if society
tries to silence them?

00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:47.690
Who loses then?

00:01:49.984 --> 00:01:52.028
- Trolling is a free speech issues,

00:01:52.028 --> 00:01:54.114
and if aren't free aren't
free to hate someone,

00:01:54.114 --> 00:01:55.824
then you're not free.

00:01:55.824 --> 00:01:57.158
- I'd been associated with all kinds of

00:01:57.158 --> 00:01:58.660
organized trolling groups.

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:01.246
We're stretching the
bounds of free speech.

00:02:01.246 --> 00:02:03.373
You either use it, or you lose it.

00:02:03.373 --> 00:02:05.166
Feelings will be hurt.

00:02:07.085 --> 00:02:08.920
- [Narrator] Politicians and the courts,

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:10.088
are now grappling,

00:02:10.088 --> 00:02:13.174
with who really is a dangerous troll.

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:16.010
- I've been called troll
by very powerful people

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:17.220
that should know better.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:19.347
Homophone, misogynist,
racist, all this stuff,

00:02:19.347 --> 00:02:23.101
is just lazy people,
not wanting to interact.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:26.396
- [Narrator] Artist Gregory Alan Elliott,

00:02:26.396 --> 00:02:29.440
is at the center of a
landmark trolling case,

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:32.986
that might land him behind
bars, and forever change,

00:02:32.986 --> 00:02:37.157
what any of us, anywhere,
can or cannot say online.

00:02:38.283 --> 00:02:40.535
- If Mr.Elliott is convicted
of a criminal offense,

00:02:40.535 --> 00:02:44.247
watch out, nobody will be
safe, you'd better shut up,

00:02:44.247 --> 00:02:48.334
because if you don't, you
could be going to jail.

00:02:53.464 --> 00:02:55.967
(loud typing)

00:02:59.971 --> 00:03:02.724
(soothing music)

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:15.236
- My name is Gina Crosley-Corcoran,

00:03:15.236 --> 00:03:17.363
I write a blog called
the Feminist Breeder,

00:03:17.363 --> 00:03:21.451
and I deal with a really steady
onslaught of very vicious,

00:03:21.451 --> 00:03:23.203
and dedicated trolls.

00:03:24.787 --> 00:03:26.789
Trolls can be really, really sneaky.

00:03:26.789 --> 00:03:28.708
When you think about patterns of stalking,

00:03:28.708 --> 00:03:31.628
stalkers don't just do it really quietly,

00:03:31.628 --> 00:03:35.215
they leave their victims clues
that they're being stalked,

00:03:35.215 --> 00:03:37.508
in order to scare them.

00:03:37.508 --> 00:03:40.637
So, today, I got a Working
Person's Store Magazine,

00:03:40.637 --> 00:03:42.722
addressed to the Feminist Breeder,

00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:44.224
with my home addressed.

00:03:44.224 --> 00:03:45.350
My trolls sent me this,

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:47.894
probably to imply that I'm
too lazy to find a real job,

00:03:47.894 --> 00:03:49.646
besides blogging, so.

00:03:55.693 --> 00:03:57.779
Guys, hey.
- Hi, mom.

00:03:57.779 --> 00:03:59.280
- Come here, pumpkin pie.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.242
- [Narrator] Gina's blog earns
her family a decent income.

00:04:02.242 --> 00:04:05.036
She writes about being a stay at home,

00:04:05.036 --> 00:04:08.831
but from a pro-choice,
modern feminist perspective,

00:04:08.831 --> 00:04:12.252
and a large active community
of like-minded women,

00:04:12.252 --> 00:04:14.629
has grown around her blog.

00:04:14.629 --> 00:04:17.257
- I like to see that recognition
response, when someone else

00:04:17.257 --> 00:04:19.008
goes, (gasps), "Me, too."

00:04:19.008 --> 00:04:20.677
And that's so validating.

00:04:20.677 --> 00:04:21.886
That's why I do it.

00:04:21.886 --> 00:04:24.806
- [Narrator] Feminists are
frequently targeted by trolls.

00:04:24.806 --> 00:04:26.766
Gina knew to expect attacks,

00:04:26.766 --> 00:04:30.436
but she's been overwhelmed
by the volume of abuse,

00:04:30.436 --> 00:04:34.607
and stunned to discover
who her trolls are.

00:04:34.607 --> 00:04:37.068
- The trolls and the
really dangerous people

00:04:37.068 --> 00:04:39.779
that I attract, are themselves mothers,

00:04:39.779 --> 00:04:41.781
and have small children at home.

00:04:41.781 --> 00:04:45.159
They get something out of
taking out their frustrations

00:04:45.159 --> 00:04:46.911
on someone like me.

00:04:46.911 --> 00:04:48.329
They see me as a figurehead, and,

00:04:48.329 --> 00:04:51.165
they're really angry about
the attention I'm getting

00:04:51.165 --> 00:04:53.835
from people, and they
want to discredit me,

00:04:53.835 --> 00:04:55.795
in any way they possibly can.

00:04:55.795 --> 00:04:58.715
These people spend all
day, taking screenshots of

00:04:58.715 --> 00:05:00.300
every word I type on the internet,

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:02.218
they all pick it apart,
they talk about my children,

00:05:02.218 --> 00:05:03.678
they talk about my clothes,

00:05:03.678 --> 00:05:05.638
they spend an incredible amount of energy,

00:05:05.638 --> 00:05:08.725
focusing exclusively on
something that they claim

00:05:08.725 --> 00:05:10.810
to completely hate.

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:12.228
And to me, that's the
difference between someone

00:05:12.228 --> 00:05:14.022
who just disagrees with you,

00:05:14.022 --> 00:05:17.567
or someone who's really
stalking every move you take.

00:05:17.567 --> 00:05:19.694
And those are the trolls.

00:05:19.694 --> 00:05:21.779
- [Narrator] Gina sees
little difference between

00:05:21.779 --> 00:05:23.781
her trolls and her stalker.

00:05:23.781 --> 00:05:25.325
They want to see her react,

00:05:25.325 --> 00:05:27.952
and they know how to push her buttons.

00:05:27.952 --> 00:05:30.955
- A stalker wants to upset that person,

00:05:30.955 --> 00:05:32.540
they want to make them afraid,

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:34.083
and that's what these trolls do,

00:05:34.083 --> 00:05:38.212
they leave comments in
such a way that makes you

00:05:38.212 --> 00:05:40.381
feel afraid of what
they're gonna do, and say.

00:05:40.381 --> 00:05:43.801
They'll leave comments about
my address, or my kids,

00:05:43.801 --> 00:05:46.012
and they do it, because
they wanna get a reaction

00:05:46.012 --> 00:05:47.013
outta me.

00:05:47.013 --> 00:05:49.891
They wanna see me lash out,
and it all feeds into this

00:05:49.891 --> 00:05:53.644
sick and twisted
entertainment value to them.

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:02.362
- [Narrator] So, what is
it, that motivates trolls?

00:06:02.362 --> 00:06:05.907
In London, England,
psychologist Raj Persaud,

00:06:05.907 --> 00:06:09.410
believes the internet has
spawned an entire new,

00:06:09.410 --> 00:06:13.331
yet unexplored set of human behaviors.

00:06:13.331 --> 00:06:14.916
- Psychologists, or psychiatrists,

00:06:14.916 --> 00:06:17.877
have been someone taken
back, by the huge amount

00:06:17.877 --> 00:06:20.213
of negativity there appears
to be on the internet.

00:06:20.213 --> 00:06:23.174
And it maybe that the
internet has unleased,

00:06:23.174 --> 00:06:26.594
a kind of dark demon within
millions of people out there,

00:06:26.594 --> 00:06:28.096
in the general public.

00:06:28.096 --> 00:06:31.182
We think that one of the
things that's problematic

00:06:31.182 --> 00:06:34.185
about the internet, is it normalizes

00:06:34.185 --> 00:06:35.311
extreme behavior.

00:06:35.311 --> 00:06:37.855
So, because you see a lot
of people on the internet

00:06:37.855 --> 00:06:39.774
behaving in an extreme fashion,

00:06:39.774 --> 00:06:43.236
you think it's more normal,
than it might actually be.

00:06:43.236 --> 00:06:45.196
And the other thing is,
is it seems to encourage

00:06:45.196 --> 00:06:47.031
a heard-like piece of behavior.

00:06:47.031 --> 00:06:49.283
If one person starts being negative,

00:06:49.283 --> 00:06:51.536
and a group of people pile in behind them,

00:06:51.536 --> 00:06:53.788
there's a kind of cascade effect,

00:06:53.788 --> 00:06:55.623
which you don't see in ordinary life,

00:06:55.623 --> 00:06:57.625
outside of the internet.

00:07:00.336 --> 00:07:02.004
- [Narrator] Psychologists truly believe,

00:07:02.004 --> 00:07:04.757
that one thing has compounded the effects,

00:07:04.757 --> 00:07:06.592
and numbers of trolls.

00:07:09.637 --> 00:07:11.514
- Anonymity seems to be
a very important factor

00:07:11.514 --> 00:07:13.599
on the internet, in that it seems to

00:07:13.599 --> 00:07:17.437
make people less inhibited
about doing nasty things.

00:07:17.437 --> 00:07:18.938
It means that

00:07:18.938 --> 00:07:21.107
people feel free to get
away with bad behavior,

00:07:21.107 --> 00:07:23.109
because they think they're not
gonna get called to account,

00:07:23.109 --> 00:07:25.736
no one's gonna point the finger at them.

00:07:25.736 --> 00:07:28.114
So, the anonymity, in
fact, seems very important,

00:07:28.114 --> 00:07:31.284
and may explain, why so
much dark, and negative,

00:07:31.284 --> 00:07:33.786
and extreme behavior
occurs on the internet,

00:07:33.786 --> 00:07:36.998
but doesn't seem to occur in real-life,

00:07:36.998 --> 00:07:39.959
in face-to-face interactions.

00:07:39.959 --> 00:07:42.086
- [Narrator] But what inspires a troll?

00:07:42.086 --> 00:07:44.505
What do they get out of it?

00:07:44.505 --> 00:07:48.634
Operating out of an undisclosed
location in North America,

00:07:48.634 --> 00:07:53.055
one hardcore, and self-professed
troll, agreed to tell us,

00:07:53.055 --> 00:07:56.642
provided we maintain his anonymity.

00:07:56.642 --> 00:07:59.145
- I'm wild goose, and I'm a member

00:07:59.145 --> 00:08:00.980
of the Bill Waggoner crew,

00:08:00.980 --> 00:08:03.983
and I've done a podcast
for the past several years,

00:08:03.983 --> 00:08:06.527
documenting internet troll activity,

00:08:06.527 --> 00:08:10.281
hacker activity, Gamer
Gate, Anonymous, Occupy,

00:08:10.281 --> 00:08:13.284
you name it, I've been a part of it.

00:08:14.494 --> 00:08:17.747
There's a serious darkness in not only us,

00:08:17.747 --> 00:08:19.832
but I think in everybody online.

00:08:19.832 --> 00:08:21.876
I think a lot of people
just don't tap into the

00:08:21.876 --> 00:08:26.005
the power of anonymity and
what it can do for them.

00:08:27.006 --> 00:08:28.549
When you're anonymous,

00:08:28.549 --> 00:08:30.885
you're free to do and say a lot things,

00:08:30.885 --> 00:08:34.347
that you otherwise
wouldn't do in real-life.

00:08:34.347 --> 00:08:37.350
You would be powerless
to do, in real-life.

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:41.229
Goose joined forces with like
minded trolls he met online,

00:08:41.229 --> 00:08:42.438
and they work as a team,

00:08:42.438 --> 00:08:46.567
to offend and infuriate as
many people as they can.

00:08:47.777 --> 00:08:50.196
- If you were to Google
"Bill Waggoner Crew",

00:08:50.196 --> 00:08:53.115
chances are, you've come
across a term called

00:08:53.115 --> 00:08:54.325
R.I.P. Trolling.

00:08:54.325 --> 00:08:59.163
When somebody dies, and their
family puts up a memorial,

00:08:59.163 --> 00:09:00.373
up in their honor,

00:09:00.373 --> 00:09:02.041
we might set up a separate page,

00:09:02.041 --> 00:09:03.751
or we might just go on that page,

00:09:03.751 --> 00:09:05.336
and create a ruckus.

00:09:05.336 --> 00:09:08.631
But the idea is to get as
many followers as possible

00:09:08.631 --> 00:09:11.551
on that page, and make it
seem as legit as possible,

00:09:11.551 --> 00:09:15.304
so that we can offend as
many people as possible,

00:09:15.304 --> 00:09:16.597
when we start posting more,

00:09:16.597 --> 00:09:19.183
other kinds of shocking material.

00:09:19.183 --> 00:09:21.936
(dramatic music)

00:09:24.355 --> 00:09:26.857
(church bell)

00:09:29.193 --> 00:09:32.530
- [Narrator] Hounding people
when they are most vulnerable.

00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:35.032
Like at the time of a loved one's funeral,

00:09:35.032 --> 00:09:37.493
is a sure-fire hit for the troll.

00:09:37.493 --> 00:09:41.664
- That's the easiest way for
us to create a mass effect,

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:44.208
is really effective when you have

00:09:44.208 --> 00:09:47.253
all these people who
are already emotional,

00:09:47.253 --> 00:09:49.171
we drop this bomb on them,

00:09:49.171 --> 00:09:52.383
with the gore photos, maybe some kind of

00:09:52.383 --> 00:09:54.010
sexually explicit photos.

00:09:54.010 --> 00:09:57.346
It sends their emotions into over drive.

00:10:00.224 --> 00:10:01.809
- [Narrator] But what do the trolls get,

00:10:01.809 --> 00:10:05.229
out of seeing these distraught reactions?

00:10:08.274 --> 00:10:12.528
Lulz. L-U-L-Z.

00:10:12.528 --> 00:10:14.113
That's the currency of the troll,

00:10:14.113 --> 00:10:16.157
and it's what they're after.

00:10:16.157 --> 00:10:19.285
Lulz, or laughs, fun and amusement,

00:10:19.285 --> 00:10:21.203
at the expense of others.

00:10:21.203 --> 00:10:24.790
The Waggoner Crew, will prod
people online for reactions.

00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:27.710
The bigger their
response, the more popular

00:10:27.710 --> 00:10:30.046
the target becomes.

00:10:30.046 --> 00:10:32.173
- Someone who gets really animated,

00:10:32.173 --> 00:10:35.635
and waves their arms around on camera,

00:10:35.635 --> 00:10:38.804
preferably, or they'll into
scream into a microphone

00:10:38.804 --> 00:10:39.972
on a podcast,

00:10:39.972 --> 00:10:42.767
they might start crying,
they become Lulz Cow.

00:10:42.767 --> 00:10:44.685
They're like an ongoing target.

00:10:44.685 --> 00:10:46.437
We love that kind of reaction.

00:10:46.437 --> 00:10:50.524
We wanna make them cry,
we love to make them cry.

00:10:51.776 --> 00:10:54.070
- [Narrator] The open
internet is a playground,

00:10:54.070 --> 00:10:56.697
for Goose, and trolls like them.

00:10:56.697 --> 00:10:59.408
And the stark contrast, to
what they can get away with

00:10:59.408 --> 00:11:02.411
in normal society, is not lost on them.

00:11:02.411 --> 00:11:06.957
- I really can't, I mean,
the real world's so boring,

00:11:06.957 --> 00:11:09.794
and it's lacking in lulz, it really is.

00:11:09.794 --> 00:11:11.921
I don't think we'll be able
to find this in real life.

00:11:11.921 --> 00:11:14.215
The internet presented
something that was never

00:11:14.215 --> 00:11:15.758
available to us before.

00:11:15.758 --> 00:11:18.427
(trumpet music)

00:11:27.978 --> 00:11:29.897
- [Narrator] But today,
if you go on the internet,

00:11:29.897 --> 00:11:33.150
you're entering the domain of the troll.

00:11:33.150 --> 00:11:37.363
But where did internet trolling all start?

00:11:37.363 --> 00:11:40.157
This unassuming, computer
science professor,

00:11:40.157 --> 00:11:42.952
may hold the answer, and may himself,

00:11:42.952 --> 00:11:47.289
be one of the very first
trolls, on the internet.

00:11:47.289 --> 00:11:49.709
- I am doctor Adam Steele,
I am an associated professor

00:11:49.709 --> 00:11:52.336
of Computer Science at
DePaul University in Chicago.

00:11:52.336 --> 00:11:56.966
I think I am the first record
trolls on the internet.

00:11:56.966 --> 00:11:58.968
Certainly not the first
troll, but the first one

00:11:58.968 --> 00:12:00.428
that was actually written up.

00:12:00.428 --> 00:12:02.847
This is piece that is here
because my wife hates,

00:12:02.847 --> 00:12:03.973
called the Alchemist.

00:12:03.973 --> 00:12:06.434
I didn't really notice, but
it sure does look like a troll

00:12:06.434 --> 00:12:09.353
and I guess he's stirring the pot.

00:12:09.353 --> 00:12:11.272
- [Narrator] Adam will
first access the internet,

00:12:11.272 --> 00:12:13.899
while attending university,
in the province of

00:12:13.899 --> 00:12:15.568
Quebec, Canada.

00:12:15.568 --> 00:12:18.696
And it wouldn't be long,
until he was stirring the pot,

00:12:18.696 --> 00:12:21.282
with his wry sense of humor.

00:12:21.282 --> 00:12:25.119
- I was a grad student in
1993, at Concordia University

00:12:25.119 --> 00:12:26.871
in Montreal, essentially, at that time,

00:12:26.871 --> 00:12:28.372
the internet was still very nascent,

00:12:28.372 --> 00:12:32.376
and at that point, was just
a relatively small number of

00:12:32.376 --> 00:12:34.503
universities that were connected together.

00:12:34.503 --> 00:12:37.923
And there was this kind of,
quasi bulletin board system,

00:12:37.923 --> 00:12:39.467
called the News Network.

00:12:39.467 --> 00:12:42.762
You had, for example,
a group like rec.humor,

00:12:42.762 --> 00:12:43.929
people would trade jokes.

00:12:43.929 --> 00:12:47.308
And I guess what happened
at some point, was that,

00:12:47.308 --> 00:12:50.644
there were people on rec.humor,
who had a certain penchant

00:12:50.644 --> 00:12:53.189
for tasteless jokes
that was not appreciated

00:12:53.189 --> 00:12:55.357
by the rest of the community, and so,

00:12:55.357 --> 00:12:58.778
the tasteless denizens
were kind of hived off,

00:12:58.778 --> 00:13:00.738
and started their own group.

00:13:00.738 --> 00:13:03.073
And, alt.tasteless was born.

00:13:05.534 --> 00:13:08.621
- [Narrator] As the chat
group alt.tasteless got going,

00:13:08.621 --> 00:13:12.625
the jokes started getting
more and more extreme,

00:13:13.626 --> 00:13:17.797
but soon, the jokes just couldn't
get the rise they wanted.

00:13:19.215 --> 00:13:21.675
- I think it was a long, hot summer, and

00:13:21.675 --> 00:13:24.136
everybody was kind of
getting a little bored,

00:13:24.136 --> 00:13:28.015
and there was this idea that
the people of alt.tasteless,

00:13:28.015 --> 00:13:32.186
should invade a newsgroup,
and somebody suggested

00:13:32.186 --> 00:13:34.522
rec.pets.cats.

00:13:34.522 --> 00:13:38.192
- [Narrator] Yes, cat lovers,
would be the next target,

00:13:38.192 --> 00:13:40.820
and Adam would be the lead.

00:13:40.820 --> 00:13:43.364
- Like any community, they
wanted to discuss problems

00:13:43.364 --> 00:13:46.534
that their cats had, so I
guess my contribution was,

00:13:46.534 --> 00:13:50.204
that we should actually
engage them, by writing

00:13:50.204 --> 00:13:53.999
a story that would appear
to be from somebody who was

00:13:53.999 --> 00:13:56.919
genuinely concerned, get them to respond.

00:13:56.919 --> 00:13:58.629
A stealth attack.

00:13:58.629 --> 00:14:00.339
So, I created a fictitious identity,

00:14:00.339 --> 00:14:04.176
and it was posted
anonymously to rec.pets.cats.

00:14:04.176 --> 00:14:06.095
- [Narrator] And what happened next,

00:14:06.095 --> 00:14:09.723
would be recognized by
Wired magazine, as something

00:14:09.723 --> 00:14:13.477
that had never happened
on the internet before.

00:14:13.477 --> 00:14:16.564
- I have two cats. Sootikin,
two year old female,

00:14:16.564 --> 00:14:17.565
and Choad.

00:14:17.565 --> 00:14:20.067
Choad's problem is that he
has really stinky shits,

00:14:20.067 --> 00:14:21.110
paint peelers.

00:14:21.110 --> 00:14:24.196
- [Narrator] Adam's post was
vulgar, and over the top,

00:14:24.196 --> 00:14:26.657
but he took care to keep it believable.

00:14:26.657 --> 00:14:28.409
- Sooti goes into heat something fierce,

00:14:28.409 --> 00:14:30.327
yowling, and presenting all the time.

00:14:30.327 --> 00:14:32.371
While dinner was cooking,
I tried to stimulate her

00:14:32.371 --> 00:14:34.540
with a Q-tip, because I had heard that

00:14:34.540 --> 00:14:36.041
one can induce ovulation that way.

00:14:36.041 --> 00:14:38.878
By date came into the bathroom
while I was doing this,

00:14:38.878 --> 00:14:40.212
and needless to say,

00:14:40.212 --> 00:14:42.381
I don't think she bought my explanation.

00:14:42.381 --> 00:14:44.466
- [Narrator] The cat lovers, however,

00:14:44.466 --> 00:14:48.554
bought Adam's story,
hook, like and sinker.

00:14:48.554 --> 00:14:51.265
- Initial responses to the
post were very helpful,

00:14:51.265 --> 00:14:55.519
and essentially suggested
that Sooti should get spayed.

00:14:55.519 --> 00:15:00.316
Then slowly, other suggestions
started popping up.

00:15:00.316 --> 00:15:03.903
- [Narrator] As the off-color
suggestions became grosser,

00:15:03.903 --> 00:15:08.657
and more extreme, the
cat lovers were outraged,

00:15:08.657 --> 00:15:12.036
and the alt.tasteless gang, had a blast.

00:15:12.036 --> 00:15:15.122
- So, what we did was,
we essentially trolled,

00:15:15.122 --> 00:15:18.417
you know, in the way you throw
a liver out behind a boat,

00:15:18.417 --> 00:15:22.254
and motor over the fish,
until somebody bites.

00:15:23.422 --> 00:15:26.008
- [Narrator] Adam, and
the alt.tasteless crew,

00:15:26.008 --> 00:15:29.970
had become the Internet's
first gang of trolls.

00:15:29.970 --> 00:15:31.472
(laughter)

00:15:31.472 --> 00:15:34.808
Back then, Adam was one of
about 14 million internet

00:15:34.808 --> 00:15:36.518
users, worldwide.

00:15:36.518 --> 00:15:40.773
25 years later, there are
three billion people online.

00:15:40.773 --> 00:15:43.233
And the nature of trolling has evolved.

00:15:43.233 --> 00:15:46.570
- What's happened now is
that trolling has taken on

00:15:46.570 --> 00:15:49.865
a meaning different from
sort of the fishing metaphor.

00:15:49.865 --> 00:15:52.159
And more towards that of
the troll under the bridge,

00:15:52.159 --> 00:15:54.453
where someone will post something,

00:15:54.453 --> 00:15:57.581
and the responses will
be very destructive,

00:15:57.581 --> 00:16:01.669
basically laying waste
to whatever's bieng said.

00:16:01.669 --> 00:16:05.589
- [Narrator] In the early
1990s, Adam and his friends,

00:16:05.589 --> 00:16:07.466
were trolling for fun.

00:16:07.466 --> 00:16:11.637
But now, negativity is
everywhere on the internet.

00:16:12.888 --> 00:16:15.307
Back in London, doctor Raj Persaud,

00:16:15.307 --> 00:16:18.185
suspects that we all
have a potential troll,

00:16:18.185 --> 00:16:20.104
lucking in our psyches.

00:16:22.064 --> 00:16:23.607
- The answer to the question
of whether we're all

00:16:23.607 --> 00:16:25.567
potential trolls, is delivered by

00:16:25.567 --> 00:16:28.988
a simple question, "Have you
ever gossiped negatively,

00:16:28.988 --> 00:16:31.198
"about someone else?"

00:16:31.198 --> 00:16:33.701
'Cause if you have, it
is a sense in which you

00:16:33.701 --> 00:16:36.120
were doing a kind of
micro-trolling behavior.

00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:39.915
- [Narrator] It's not pretty,
but science has now proved,

00:16:39.915 --> 00:16:43.711
that putting someone else
down, can make us feel better,

00:16:43.711 --> 00:16:44.670
about ourselves.

00:16:44.670 --> 00:16:49.133
And there's a specific name,
for the feeling that results.

00:16:49.133 --> 00:16:51.427
- The psychologists are
taking increasing interest,

00:16:51.427 --> 00:16:53.721
in what's called the Schadenfreude effect.

00:16:53.721 --> 00:16:55.848
And Schadenfreude is a word from German,

00:16:55.848 --> 00:17:00.019
which is about feeling pleasure
at someone else's downfall.

00:17:02.980 --> 00:17:04.148
There're a lot of people on the planet,

00:17:04.148 --> 00:17:06.817
feeling low self esteem,
feeling beaten up by life.

00:17:06.817 --> 00:17:09.486
Maybe watching other people doing badly,

00:17:09.486 --> 00:17:11.947
watching other people
suffer, seems to correct

00:17:11.947 --> 00:17:13.699
your low self-esteem.

00:17:14.950 --> 00:17:17.202
- [Narrator] Deriving pleasure
from the pain of others,

00:17:17.202 --> 00:17:21.540
is a character we all
seem to have, to a degree.

00:17:21.540 --> 00:17:23.751
But psychologists are now finding,

00:17:23.751 --> 00:17:26.920
that hardcore trolls,
are likely to express,

00:17:26.920 --> 00:17:30.049
four dark personality traits.

00:17:30.049 --> 00:17:34.470
In Winnipeg, Canada, a landmark
study of internet trolls,

00:17:34.470 --> 00:17:37.556
has now shown that one
of those dark traits,

00:17:37.556 --> 00:17:39.391
stands above the rest.

00:17:40.559 --> 00:17:41.769
Sadism.

00:17:41.769 --> 00:17:43.771
- Sadists do it because they like.

00:17:43.771 --> 00:17:47.232
Some people get their jollies
by being rude, and obnoxious,

00:17:47.232 --> 00:17:48.984
and causing hard to harm to other people.

00:17:48.984 --> 00:17:51.111
Sometimes, serious harm to other people,

00:17:51.111 --> 00:17:52.696
and it gives them pleasure.

00:17:52.696 --> 00:17:55.741
- [Narrator] Doctor Paul
Trapnell, and his colleagues,

00:17:55.741 --> 00:17:58.368
have confirmed, that the
internet, with millions of

00:17:58.368 --> 00:18:02.122
potential victims, and
anonymity to hide behind,

00:18:02.122 --> 00:18:06.001
is a playground for people
with sadistic tendencies.

00:18:06.001 --> 00:18:08.170
- It's a venue where
a person can go on and

00:18:08.170 --> 00:18:11.423
ruffle somebody's feathers, or
embarrass or humiliate them,

00:18:11.423 --> 00:18:15.135
or make them feel anguish,
and there is no consequence

00:18:15.135 --> 00:18:17.387
to that, and it's easy to do.

00:18:17.387 --> 00:18:18.847
So that might attack people

00:18:18.847 --> 00:18:20.682
that take pleasure in harming people,

00:18:20.682 --> 00:18:24.061
and that seems to be what we found.

00:18:24.061 --> 00:18:26.688
- [Narrator] Trapnell and
his team, began their studies

00:18:26.688 --> 00:18:29.274
by focusing on people who
frequently leave comments

00:18:29.274 --> 00:18:32.653
on message boards, a
significant number of them,

00:18:32.653 --> 00:18:35.239
unabashedly rated trolling,

00:18:35.239 --> 00:18:37.991
as their favorite commenting activity.

00:18:37.991 --> 00:18:40.953
- Then we asked them to
also fill out a number of

00:18:40.953 --> 00:18:42.037
personality questionnaires,

00:18:42.037 --> 00:18:45.541
and those questionnaires
included some measures of

00:18:45.541 --> 00:18:50.170
well-known, widely, studied,
forms of nasty tendencies.

00:18:50.170 --> 00:18:52.506
We found, as expected, that the trollers,

00:18:52.506 --> 00:18:55.050
were highers on of those
in the level of traits.

00:18:55.050 --> 00:18:57.469
It's not a stretch, but
nobody had investigated that,

00:18:57.469 --> 00:18:59.388
with data, and so we did so,

00:18:59.388 --> 00:19:01.807
and sure enough, that's true.

00:19:09.064 --> 00:19:09.898
Let me illustrate what I mean

00:19:09.898 --> 00:19:12.359
by these four, dark tendencies,

00:19:12.359 --> 00:19:14.945
that have especially
toxic social consequences.

00:19:14.945 --> 00:19:18.657
Psychopathy, we can be
callously indifferent,

00:19:18.657 --> 00:19:22.578
by our reckless pursuit
of things that we want.

00:19:27.666 --> 00:19:30.252
In narcissism, we are callously
indifferent to others,

00:19:30.252 --> 00:19:32.045
by our pursuit of grandiosity,

00:19:32.045 --> 00:19:33.797
of wanting to hog the limelight,

00:19:33.797 --> 00:19:37.968
wanting to think that we are
superior to other people.

00:19:40.053 --> 00:19:43.974
Machiavellianism, the
pursuit of strategic harm,

00:19:43.974 --> 00:19:46.476
in order to benefit ourselves.

00:19:48.896 --> 00:19:51.481
Sadism is a particular
malevolent way that we can do

00:19:51.481 --> 00:19:55.569
harm to others, and that is
by seeking to harm people

00:19:55.569 --> 00:19:59.072
purposely, because it's pleasurable to us.

00:20:00.699 --> 00:20:03.243
So, all of us have the
potential to be dark in these

00:20:03.243 --> 00:20:05.454
four different ways, and indeed,

00:20:05.454 --> 00:20:08.290
some people combine these
tendencies in ways that make

00:20:08.290 --> 00:20:12.044
them especially dark,
malevolent individuals.

00:20:13.128 --> 00:20:16.131
And that is, a very,
very malevolent person.

00:20:16.131 --> 00:20:18.884
This is not somebody you
wanna have anything to do with

00:20:18.884 --> 00:20:20.802
at all, definitely not.

00:20:25.557 --> 00:20:28.352
- [Narrator] Nasty individuals
have had free reign,

00:20:28.352 --> 00:20:31.730
on the internet, and
trolling is on the rise.

00:20:31.730 --> 00:20:35.901
But more and more people,
are starting to fight back.

00:20:40.197 --> 00:20:43.200
This accused troll, has
been forced off the internet

00:20:43.200 --> 00:20:46.370
for three years, by a Toronto court.

00:20:46.370 --> 00:20:50.415
And today, his artwork has been
forced off these cafe walls,

00:20:50.415 --> 00:20:51.917
by angry protests.

00:20:53.961 --> 00:20:56.838
Gregory Allen Elliott, is in limbo,

00:20:56.838 --> 00:21:01.510
awaiting verdict, in a landmark
Twitter harassment trial.

00:21:01.510 --> 00:21:03.804
When I use Twitter, I
use it at as pure form.

00:21:03.804 --> 00:21:05.806
As an artist, I explain
that is is a brush,

00:21:05.806 --> 00:21:08.350
this is some paint, this is a canvas.

00:21:08.350 --> 00:21:10.644
I used it comment on politics,

00:21:10.644 --> 00:21:12.396
I used it to help people.

00:21:12.396 --> 00:21:14.564
But I just used it everyway possible.

00:21:14.564 --> 00:21:17.401
One day, I responded to
every tweet that came across

00:21:17.401 --> 00:21:18.402
my feed stream.

00:21:18.402 --> 00:21:19.319
And then someone said,

00:21:19.319 --> 00:21:20.904
"You don't have to respond to everything."

00:21:20.904 --> 00:21:21.905
And I said, "I know."

00:21:21.905 --> 00:21:23.782
Right, but today I'm doing,
that's what I'm doing,

00:21:23.782 --> 00:21:26.994
as an artist and an experimentor.

00:21:26.994 --> 00:21:28.954
- [Narrator] At one
point, Gregory was sending

00:21:28.954 --> 00:21:30.539
300 tweets per day.

00:21:31.999 --> 00:21:33.959
It was ascorbic, sometimes crude,

00:21:33.959 --> 00:21:36.044
and very opinionated.

00:21:36.044 --> 00:21:38.213
Inevitably, he made some enemies,

00:21:38.213 --> 00:21:40.382
who wanted to shut him up.

00:21:41.383 --> 00:21:43.093
Chris Murphy, Gregory's lawyer,

00:21:43.093 --> 00:21:45.387
explains how Gregory
went from volunteering,

00:21:45.387 --> 00:21:47.597
to design a logo for
feminist organization,

00:21:47.597 --> 00:21:51.435
to being arrested, and
jailed, for three days.

00:21:51.435 --> 00:21:54.521
- When Greg called me, he had
no idea why the police were

00:21:54.521 --> 00:21:56.940
there, and neither did I, obviously,

00:21:56.940 --> 00:22:00.068
and it wasn't until,
sometime after this arrest,

00:22:00.068 --> 00:22:02.654
when he was in custody,
that he first learned,

00:22:02.654 --> 00:22:04.448
what the police were doing that.

00:22:04.448 --> 00:22:07.034
And it turned out, there was a woman named

00:22:07.034 --> 00:22:10.037
Steph Gutharie, who had
said that Mr. Elliott,

00:22:10.037 --> 00:22:12.122
had been criminally
harassing her on Twitter.

00:22:12.122 --> 00:22:15.625
The medium that Mr.Eliott
is alleged to have harassed,

00:22:15.625 --> 00:22:18.003
the complain that Miss
Guthrie is on Twitter,

00:22:18.003 --> 00:22:19.046
that's it.

00:22:19.046 --> 00:22:23.175
No personal emails, not
Facebook, nothing in person,

00:22:23.175 --> 00:22:26.845
no in-person meetings,
no stalking behavior.

00:22:28.430 --> 00:22:30.265
- Here is the one they threw coffee on.

00:22:30.265 --> 00:22:33.060
You can throw coffee on it,
but creating the picture's

00:22:33.060 --> 00:22:35.645
worth more than a thousand
people destroying it.

00:22:35.645 --> 00:22:37.356
This is like freedom of speech,

00:22:37.356 --> 00:22:39.733
this is saying, you
know, like I can comment,

00:22:39.733 --> 00:22:41.818
on what you're doing.

00:22:41.818 --> 00:22:44.154
As long it's not a threat.

00:22:44.154 --> 00:22:47.657
If the truth hurts you, it's not problem,

00:22:47.657 --> 00:22:50.577
because I'm just telling the truth.

00:22:51.536 --> 00:22:53.413
- Mr. Elliott was disagreeing with

00:22:53.413 --> 00:22:55.207
Stephanie Guthrie's politics,

00:22:55.207 --> 00:22:58.043
she's a founder of a political
organization in Toronto,

00:22:58.043 --> 00:23:00.337
called Women in Toronto Politics.

00:23:00.337 --> 00:23:04.341
Mr. Elliott was an ally of
hers, for a number of months.

00:23:04.341 --> 00:23:07.803
But then, there was a
break in the relationship,

00:23:07.803 --> 00:23:11.848
because, Mr. Elliott, stuck
up for someone he believed was

00:23:11.848 --> 00:23:16.019
being bullied by Miss Guthrie
and her friends, on Twitter.

00:23:17.229 --> 00:23:19.815
- [Narrator] Miss Guthrie,
who refused to be interview

00:23:19.815 --> 00:23:22.442
for this film, asked
her followers to quote,

00:23:22.442 --> 00:23:25.570
"Tip the internet, on a
young man who had made

00:23:25.570 --> 00:23:28.740
"an anti-feminist video
game, a game that even

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:32.160
"Mr. Elliott admits
was clearly offensive."

00:23:32.160 --> 00:23:36.957
- It was an orchestrated and
dangerous attack on this guy.

00:23:36.957 --> 00:23:39.918
Right up to that point, I was
sending tweets to them saying,

00:23:39.918 --> 00:23:43.422
"Look, the guy only has
11 followers, ignore it,

00:23:43.422 --> 00:23:47.717
"it's not good branding,
to destroy people's lives."

00:23:47.717 --> 00:23:49.010
But they said, "Screw you, Greg."

00:23:49.010 --> 00:23:51.430
And I said, "Well, you
have to take me seriously.

00:23:51.430 --> 00:23:53.682
"You're not gonna stop."

00:23:53.682 --> 00:23:56.852
I sent a tweet, that I'm
gonna paraphrase it, it said,

00:23:56.852 --> 00:24:00.105
"If he commits suicide, I'm
holding you all responsible."

00:24:00.105 --> 00:24:03.567
And at that point, my helping stopped,

00:24:03.567 --> 00:24:07.404
and the attacks started, and
I had to start defending.

00:24:07.404 --> 00:24:10.282
- [Narrator] Gregory didn't
know what he was up against.

00:24:10.282 --> 00:24:13.118
A community had labeled
him a troll, and they were

00:24:13.118 --> 00:24:15.704
determined to get rid of him.

00:24:15.704 --> 00:24:17.372
- I knew they were trying to
get me kicked off Twitter.

00:24:17.372 --> 00:24:21.001
So, at first it was a
campaign of intimidation.

00:24:21.001 --> 00:24:23.670
Second, it was, fake identities.

00:24:24.796 --> 00:24:27.048
Using my face, and names that
were similar to my names,

00:24:27.048 --> 00:24:29.634
and that's illegal, and
if I had felt like it,

00:24:29.634 --> 00:24:32.220
I could've got whoever did
it, kicked off Twitter.

00:24:32.220 --> 00:24:34.890
And then it was hastag, GAEhole.

00:24:35.765 --> 00:24:38.226
Gregory Allen Elliott
Asshole. So, GAEhole.

00:24:38.226 --> 00:24:41.104
So, this was an orchestrated
campaign, that ramped up.

00:24:41.104 --> 00:24:45.108
- [Narrator] Gregory fought
back with tweets that were

00:24:45.108 --> 00:24:47.110
often abrasive and confrontational,

00:24:47.110 --> 00:24:50.947
but he never broke
Twitter's terms of service.

00:24:50.947 --> 00:24:52.741
That hasn't stopped the justice system

00:24:52.741 --> 00:24:55.243
from charging him with
criminal harassment,

00:24:55.243 --> 00:24:58.455
and imposing bail conditions,
taking away his internet use

00:24:58.455 --> 00:24:59.789
for three years.

00:25:01.166 --> 00:25:02.709
- So, what I've had to do for

00:25:02.709 --> 00:25:05.462
for the three years, to,

00:25:05.462 --> 00:25:07.255
because I was banned from the internet,

00:25:07.255 --> 00:25:10.425
and banned from email, is
I've had to rely on family

00:25:10.425 --> 00:25:14.221
and friends, and try and cut
into their lives and schedules.

00:25:14.221 --> 00:25:18.141
Yeah, to be forced back
onto a pencil and eraser is

00:25:18.141 --> 00:25:19.601
very interesting.

00:25:19.601 --> 00:25:21.728
- I've spent, I couldn't even count,

00:25:21.728 --> 00:25:26.483
thousands of hours, like
this, sitting like this,

00:25:26.483 --> 00:25:31.196
out of my own time, because
he was not able to do his own

00:25:31.196 --> 00:25:34.366
evidence gather for his case.

00:25:34.366 --> 00:25:35.283
- So, you have to do,

00:25:35.283 --> 00:25:37.285
the most painful thing
is having to go through

00:25:37.285 --> 00:25:39.663
stream of consciousness,
blather of Twitter.

00:25:39.663 --> 00:25:41.623
Not exciting for me, and I can imagine,

00:25:41.623 --> 00:25:42.457
it's even less exciting

00:25:42.457 --> 00:25:46.586
for everyone that was helping me out, so.

00:25:46.586 --> 00:25:49.005
- [Narrator] Sifting through
the intricate web of thousands

00:25:49.005 --> 00:25:52.592
of tweets and retweets, made
one thing abundantly clear

00:25:52.592 --> 00:25:55.178
to Gregory's lawyer, Chris Murphy.

00:25:55.178 --> 00:25:58.557
- Gregory Allen Elliott is not a troll.

00:25:58.557 --> 00:25:59.558
There's no way.

00:25:59.558 --> 00:26:02.978
He's either communicating
a political message,

00:26:02.978 --> 00:26:07.190
or he's defending himself
against their slander.

00:26:07.190 --> 00:26:09.776
Those two things do not make you a troll.

00:26:09.776 --> 00:26:11.820
He wasn't going near them, physically,

00:26:11.820 --> 00:26:13.488
wasn't saying anything threatening,

00:26:13.488 --> 00:26:15.490
wasn't saying anything sexual to them.

00:26:15.490 --> 00:26:17.909
He was communicating about politics.

00:26:17.909 --> 00:26:21.913
And he was following the rules on Twitter.

00:26:21.913 --> 00:26:24.249
- [Narrator] But because
Gregory wouldn't shut up,

00:26:24.249 --> 00:26:26.793
his accusers went to
the police, saying that

00:26:26.793 --> 00:26:29.212
they felt threatened.

00:26:29.212 --> 00:26:32.966
Now, Gregory waits for the
judge to decide his fate.

00:26:32.966 --> 00:26:35.802
Freedom or six months behind bars.

00:26:37.220 --> 00:26:39.723
- If Mr. Elliott is convicted
of a criminal offense,

00:26:39.723 --> 00:26:40.849
watch out.

00:26:40.849 --> 00:26:43.018
Because, nobody will be safe.

00:26:43.018 --> 00:26:46.021
What you're gonna do if you're involved

00:26:46.021 --> 00:26:47.981
in a political conversation, online,

00:26:47.981 --> 00:26:50.734
and the mob comes to shut you up,

00:26:51.735 --> 00:26:53.028
you'd better shut up,

00:26:53.028 --> 00:26:55.697
because if you don't, you
could be going to jail.

00:26:55.697 --> 00:26:58.116
(door slams)

00:27:04.414 --> 00:27:07.500
- [Narrator] Internet trolling
has two essential parts.

00:27:07.500 --> 00:27:10.712
The attack, and your reaction.

00:27:10.712 --> 00:27:13.548
A big reaction is what the trolls crave,

00:27:13.548 --> 00:27:16.343
and the way you respond,
determines whether or not

00:27:16.343 --> 00:27:19.095
you are a worthwhile target.

00:27:19.095 --> 00:27:22.140
- If it's just somebody, that's
gonna, you know, block us,

00:27:22.140 --> 00:27:24.809
that's the end of the game,
battle you got sucked.

00:27:24.809 --> 00:27:27.395
That doesn't provide
too much entertainment.

00:27:27.395 --> 00:27:31.024
- [Narrator] Prolific Wild
Goose, and the Waggoner Crew,

00:27:31.024 --> 00:27:35.445
are all about the lulz, the
laughs, their victims provide.

00:27:35.445 --> 00:27:37.530
They call them Lulz Cows.

00:27:38.406 --> 00:27:42.535
So, there's a logical limit
to how far they'll push them.

00:27:42.535 --> 00:27:45.080
- There's a symbiotic
relationship between the

00:27:45.080 --> 00:27:47.624
internet troll, and the Lulz Cow.

00:27:47.624 --> 00:27:51.753
The Bill Waggoner crew has
never pushed anyone towards

00:27:51.753 --> 00:27:53.755
suicide, nor have we tried to,

00:27:53.755 --> 00:27:55.423
that's never our intent.

00:27:55.423 --> 00:27:57.842
Why would we kill a
perfectly good Lulz Cow?

00:27:57.842 --> 00:28:00.178
If we were to knock them off the internet,

00:28:00.178 --> 00:28:04.265
or out of real life, what
benefit would that provide us?

00:28:04.265 --> 00:28:06.518
How're we gonna get
any lulz our of someone

00:28:06.518 --> 00:28:08.561
who's six feet underground.

00:28:08.561 --> 00:28:10.021
You know,

00:28:10.021 --> 00:28:11.189
last I looked,

00:28:12.482 --> 00:28:15.735
dead people don't come on the internet.

00:28:24.411 --> 00:28:26.621
- [Narrator] Stay at home
mom and professional blogger,

00:28:26.621 --> 00:28:30.250
Gina Crosly-Corcoran, faces a
politically motivated faction

00:28:30.250 --> 00:28:32.460
among her trolls.

00:28:32.460 --> 00:28:35.338
They disagree with her
opinions on home birth,

00:28:35.338 --> 00:28:37.882
breast feeding, and
the health care system.

00:28:37.882 --> 00:28:39.676
And they want to drive her blog

00:28:39.676 --> 00:28:42.637
off the internet, altogether.

00:28:42.637 --> 00:28:44.806
- I have a large reach,
but I don't feel like

00:28:44.806 --> 00:28:46.391
it somehow makes me better than anybody,

00:28:46.391 --> 00:28:48.393
or makes me some sort of powerful figure,

00:28:48.393 --> 00:28:50.353
but they see it as very powerful,

00:28:50.353 --> 00:28:54.107
and that's why they want to shut it down.

00:28:54.107 --> 00:28:56.192
- [Narrator] A few years
ago, a coordinated trolling

00:28:56.192 --> 00:28:59.863
campaign, drove away
all of Gina's sponsors.

00:29:01.781 --> 00:29:03.199
- There were a couple
of brands that hung on,

00:29:03.199 --> 00:29:04.993
but ultimately they were like,

00:29:04.993 --> 00:29:07.287
"Listen, these people are
flooding our Facebook page,

00:29:07.287 --> 00:29:10.749
"with negative comments,
and hateful comments,

00:29:10.749 --> 00:29:12.459
"and talking about our families now,

00:29:12.459 --> 00:29:14.377
"and we just can't do it."

00:29:14.377 --> 00:29:18.214
So, suddenly, almost overnight,
I had no income base,

00:29:18.214 --> 00:29:21.259
but I had, what had become
an expensive blog to run.

00:29:21.259 --> 00:29:23.011
- [Narrator] Gina took a chance,

00:29:23.011 --> 00:29:25.555
and put up a paywall, and fortunately,

00:29:25.555 --> 00:29:27.599
it turned out that most of her audience,

00:29:27.599 --> 00:29:29.476
was willing to pay to be part of

00:29:29.476 --> 00:29:32.103
the Feminist Breeder community.

00:29:32.103 --> 00:29:34.939
But the frustrated trolls,
have since stooped,

00:29:34.939 --> 00:29:35.774
to new lows.

00:29:35.774 --> 00:29:36.858
(police sirens)

00:29:36.858 --> 00:29:38.610
- One of them called the police,

00:29:38.610 --> 00:29:41.821
and told them that I
was committing suicide.

00:29:41.821 --> 00:29:43.782
So the police showed up at my house,

00:29:43.782 --> 00:29:47.202
at like three in the
morning, woke up out of bed,

00:29:47.202 --> 00:29:51.372
and said, "We got an anonymous
tip, that you are suicidal.

00:29:52.665 --> 00:29:55.168
"And we have to put you
under a 72 hour psych hold."

00:29:55.168 --> 00:29:57.879
So, I was being thrown in
the back of an ambulance,

00:29:57.879 --> 00:30:02.383
crying, you know, upset,
afraid that my children

00:30:02.383 --> 00:30:05.762
were gonna wake up and see
mommy being taken away.

00:30:05.762 --> 00:30:07.597
Luckily, I was able to talk to the EMT,

00:30:07.597 --> 00:30:09.015
and I explained to him, like,

00:30:09.015 --> 00:30:12.018
"Listen, this person
you're saying from Twitter,

00:30:12.018 --> 00:30:14.896
"this person doesn't know me,
please don't take me anywhere,

00:30:14.896 --> 00:30:15.897
"I have kids up to in the morning."

00:30:15.897 --> 00:30:20.068
And luckily, he sort of saw
the situation for what it was,

00:30:20.068 --> 00:30:22.487
and he let me out of
the ambulance, and said,

00:30:22.487 --> 00:30:24.697
"Okay, go back to bed."

00:30:24.697 --> 00:30:27.033
So, I found out from the
police that anyone can

00:30:27.033 --> 00:30:29.994
make a call, and say that
they're worried about you

00:30:29.994 --> 00:30:32.121
committing suicide, or
they're worried about your

00:30:32.121 --> 00:30:34.374
children's welfare, or anyone
can make an anonymous tip,

00:30:34.374 --> 00:30:37.460
they don't have to leave their name, so,

00:30:37.460 --> 00:30:38.962
there's really nothing legally speaking

00:30:38.962 --> 00:30:41.840
that I can do about that.

00:30:41.840 --> 00:30:43.299
My trolls realized that was a good way to

00:30:43.299 --> 00:30:46.845
use my own local police to harass me,

00:30:46.845 --> 00:30:48.930
and get me out of bed
at three in the morning.

00:30:48.930 --> 00:30:51.099
(loud knocking)

00:30:51.099 --> 00:30:54.644
In the course of a year,
it happened three times.

00:30:54.644 --> 00:30:57.188
So, I went to the police, and I said,

00:30:57.188 --> 00:30:58.648
"This has to be illegal."

00:30:58.648 --> 00:31:01.401
And, you know, the local
police's response was basically,

00:31:01.401 --> 00:31:05.572
"Well, if you don't like
it, get off the internet."

00:31:09.450 --> 00:31:12.370
- [Narrator] When the police
cannot, or will not help,

00:31:12.370 --> 00:31:17.166
there is another place that
trolling victims can turn.

00:31:17.166 --> 00:31:18.001
- Nice to meet you.

00:31:18.001 --> 00:31:20.086
- Please come have a seat.

00:31:20.086 --> 00:31:22.964
The victims I deal with,
come to us, very frustrated.

00:31:22.964 --> 00:31:24.632
They're frustrated with the situation,

00:31:24.632 --> 00:31:27.093
because they feel helpless.

00:31:27.093 --> 00:31:29.929
- [Narrator] Roman Horak, is
lead forensic investigator,

00:31:29.929 --> 00:31:34.559
at Infotransic, an international
cyber security firm.

00:31:34.559 --> 00:31:37.854
He tracks down the trolls
that law enforcement won't,

00:31:37.854 --> 00:31:39.564
or can't find.

00:31:39.564 --> 00:31:42.734
- The reasons why the police
won't engage the cases,

00:31:42.734 --> 00:31:44.235
is because they won't believe

00:31:44.235 --> 00:31:46.070
they have enough of a serious threat,

00:31:46.070 --> 00:31:48.323
or they won't have, the
person themselves won't have

00:31:48.323 --> 00:31:50.158
enough evidence.

00:31:50.158 --> 00:31:53.912
- I've been getting a lot of
people threatening me online

00:31:53.912 --> 00:31:58.124
and it's scary, I'm not really
sure what to do with this.

00:31:58.124 --> 00:32:01.544
- What sites mainly are the
treats coming through on?

00:32:01.544 --> 00:32:03.588
- Mostly Twitter, but
there's couple on Facebook.

00:32:03.588 --> 00:32:04.589
- Okay.

00:32:04.589 --> 00:32:07.258
One of the big questions,
is understanding the motive

00:32:07.258 --> 00:32:08.635
of the troll.

00:32:08.635 --> 00:32:10.219
There's some trolls that they just

00:32:10.219 --> 00:32:12.972
go on the internet, looking
for someone random to,

00:32:12.972 --> 00:32:14.140
you know, harass.

00:32:14.140 --> 00:32:17.226
However, the vast majority
of trolls that we deal with

00:32:17.226 --> 00:32:19.646
are someone who they know.

00:32:19.646 --> 00:32:23.816
They would've had a relationship
somehow with the offender.

00:32:24.817 --> 00:32:27.487
- [Narrator] Indeed, in the
course of their investigations,

00:32:27.487 --> 00:32:30.073
Roman and his team have discovered that

00:32:30.073 --> 00:32:32.116
it is people we know,

00:32:32.116 --> 00:32:34.702
family, and friends, and work mates,

00:32:34.702 --> 00:32:36.704
who are most often behind,

00:32:36.704 --> 00:32:39.374
the heinous attacks we can face.

00:32:44.003 --> 00:32:46.422
But Roman's team has also uncovered a new,

00:32:46.422 --> 00:32:50.510
more dangerous type of
player in the trolling game.

00:32:50.510 --> 00:32:52.762
Professional trolls.

00:32:52.762 --> 00:32:55.056
Those who can be hired to settle a score,

00:32:55.056 --> 00:32:58.142
and attack others for money.

00:32:58.142 --> 00:33:01.312
- Online trolls for hire, this
service is available online,

00:33:01.312 --> 00:33:03.356
almost like a hitman.

00:33:03.356 --> 00:33:05.316
Some of the guys are very professional.

00:33:05.316 --> 00:33:07.485
They even create their own malware,

00:33:07.485 --> 00:33:10.488
target to that victims, so
they're right there on coding.

00:33:10.488 --> 00:33:11.990
These guys know what they're doing.

00:33:11.990 --> 00:33:13.992
These trolls for hire could be

00:33:13.992 --> 00:33:15.159
pretty much anywhere in the world.

00:33:15.159 --> 00:33:18.746
Romania, or Ukraine,
Russia, lots from there.

00:33:18.746 --> 00:33:20.665
Trynna track them down, often becomes

00:33:20.665 --> 00:33:23.334
an international operation,

00:33:23.334 --> 00:33:25.670
having to coordinate between
different law enforcements

00:33:25.670 --> 00:33:28.423
in different countries.

00:33:28.423 --> 00:33:31.926
- [Narrator] But, thankfully,
the vast majority of trolls

00:33:31.926 --> 00:33:33.761
are outright amateurs,

00:33:33.761 --> 00:33:36.681
who don't really know how
to cover their tracks.

00:33:36.681 --> 00:33:39.642
- About 85% of people
who're doing these things,

00:33:39.642 --> 00:33:42.103
you know, trolling and
harassing people online,

00:33:42.103 --> 00:33:43.980
are not tech savvy.

00:33:43.980 --> 00:33:45.148
Most offenders are leaving

00:33:45.148 --> 00:33:47.942
a vast amount of digital
evidence, everyday.

00:33:47.942 --> 00:33:50.486
I mean every second,
every minute, right now.

00:33:50.486 --> 00:33:54.657
Pretty much everything we do
online is potentially archived.

00:33:56.242 --> 00:33:57.827
- [Narrator] Many trolls operate with

00:33:57.827 --> 00:34:01.289
a false sense of security,
about their anonymity.

00:34:01.289 --> 00:34:06.210
And the potential consequences
they face, if exposed.

00:34:06.210 --> 00:34:09.255
In England, the law has
taken a hard stance,

00:34:09.255 --> 00:34:13.092
and a line has been drawn
in the digital sand.

00:34:14.218 --> 00:34:16.262
- Yeah, I sent some tweets in 2014,

00:34:16.262 --> 00:34:18.556
and end up getting 12
week prison sentence,

00:34:18.556 --> 00:34:19.974
for those tweets.

00:34:21.184 --> 00:34:24.228
- [Narrator] It wasn't her
habit to abuse people online,

00:34:24.228 --> 00:34:27.398
but today, Isabella Sorely,
is known as one of England's

00:34:27.398 --> 00:34:29.233
most notorious trolls.

00:34:30.151 --> 00:34:33.404
So, what was Isabella's online offense?

00:34:34.405 --> 00:34:38.701
In 2013, feminist activist
Caroline Criado-Perez,

00:34:38.701 --> 00:34:41.037
was campaigning to have
a woman's face appear

00:34:41.037 --> 00:34:44.624
on the next printing of
the English 10 pound note.

00:34:44.624 --> 00:34:47.668
The public reaction was largely positive.

00:34:47.668 --> 00:34:51.172
But she was also deluged
by a torrent or abusive,

00:34:51.172 --> 00:34:54.926
irrational and threatening
messages on Twitter.

00:34:54.926 --> 00:34:57.804
Isabella joined that mob.

00:34:57.804 --> 00:34:59.806
- That particular night,
I was incredibly drunk,

00:34:59.806 --> 00:35:03.017
and I decided to go home
and just send tweets.

00:35:03.017 --> 00:35:05.478
It wasn't about the
campaign, I don't overly care

00:35:05.478 --> 00:35:06.854
what's on a British bank note.

00:35:06.854 --> 00:35:08.773
I know she was campaigned,
to have a woman,

00:35:08.773 --> 00:35:10.900
and the Queen's on the
bank note anyway, so,

00:35:10.900 --> 00:35:14.362
I don't, yeah, did really not care at all,

00:35:14.362 --> 00:35:15.947
what she was campaigning about.

00:35:15.947 --> 00:35:17.990
Even now, I don't even
know why I sent them.

00:35:17.990 --> 00:35:19.992
I'd love to know why,
and I'd love to give like

00:35:19.992 --> 00:35:23.287
a clear answer, but the
real thing is just that,

00:35:23.287 --> 00:35:25.957
I was drunk and just
jumped on the bandwagon,

00:35:25.957 --> 00:35:27.667
and followed a lot of other people.

00:35:27.667 --> 00:35:30.419
- [Narrator] The trolls were not debating,

00:35:30.419 --> 00:35:32.296
they were spewing hate,

00:35:32.296 --> 00:35:34.674
and hating Criado-Perez personally,

00:35:34.674 --> 00:35:37.218
for her feminist agenda.

00:35:37.218 --> 00:35:40.429
Isabella added six
threatening tweets of her own.

00:35:40.429 --> 00:35:43.808
She threatened rape, and worse.

00:35:43.808 --> 00:35:44.809
- I woke up in the morning,

00:35:44.809 --> 00:35:48.146
obviously worse for wear,
realized what I'd done,

00:35:48.146 --> 00:35:51.065
I was physically sick, I don't
know if it was down to tweet,

00:35:51.065 --> 00:35:53.901
or just the hangover, but absolutely vile.

00:35:53.901 --> 00:35:55.528
What I had tweeted was disgusting,

00:35:55.528 --> 00:35:58.573
so, to think that I
actually typed that out,

00:35:58.573 --> 00:36:00.366
I felt disgusted in myself,

00:36:00.366 --> 00:36:04.495
that I had sent those specific
words to another person.

00:36:05.538 --> 00:36:07.331
- [Narrator] Filled with
remorse the night after

00:36:07.331 --> 00:36:09.041
spewing her online hate,

00:36:09.041 --> 00:36:12.503
Isabella took to the
internet, to both confess,

00:36:12.503 --> 00:36:14.463
and say sorry.

00:36:14.463 --> 00:36:16.465
- I apologized to her, went,

00:36:16.465 --> 00:36:19.135
"Look, it is an empty threat,
do not worry. I'm sorry."

00:36:19.135 --> 00:36:20.970
I don't know if I reassured her at all,

00:36:20.970 --> 00:36:23.764
but, kind of, it made me feel
a little bit better that,

00:36:23.764 --> 00:36:26.100
"Yeah, she's getting all these
other real death threats,

00:36:26.100 --> 00:36:28.436
"at least mine is an empty threat,

00:36:28.436 --> 00:36:30.771
"she doesn't have to worry
about me following through

00:36:30.771 --> 00:36:32.356
"with what I said."

00:36:33.733 --> 00:36:35.359
- [Narrator] But rape, and death threats,

00:36:35.359 --> 00:36:38.112
are taken very seriously.

00:36:38.112 --> 00:36:41.365
Four months later, Isabella was arrested,

00:36:41.365 --> 00:36:43.743
along with another troll.

00:36:43.743 --> 00:36:45.828
Her trial, was widely publicized,

00:36:45.828 --> 00:36:48.831
and she faced the scorn of a nation.

00:36:49.749 --> 00:36:51.709
- I got a 12 week prison sentence,

00:36:51.709 --> 00:36:54.378
in Holloway prison, it was hard.

00:36:57.590 --> 00:36:58.591
It was really hard.

00:36:58.591 --> 00:37:00.718
So obviously like how high profile it was,

00:37:00.718 --> 00:37:03.137
I couldn't hide away, I had
all the prisoners asking

00:37:03.137 --> 00:37:05.181
why I did it, I had all
like the prison guards,

00:37:05.181 --> 00:37:06.599
asking why I did it.

00:37:06.599 --> 00:37:08.434
I was getting a lot of
abuse from everywhere,

00:37:08.434 --> 00:37:10.019
I was made out to be a woman hater,

00:37:10.019 --> 00:37:12.563
and obviously, I'm in a
woman's prison, so I was like,

00:37:12.563 --> 00:37:14.649
"Oh dear, now I got my
head kicked in, because

00:37:14.649 --> 00:37:17.109
"what someone had saw what
I'd tweeted on the news."

00:37:17.109 --> 00:37:20.029
I saw myself on crime
watch in the prison cells,

00:37:20.029 --> 00:37:21.447
so that was hard.

00:37:23.491 --> 00:37:25.409
- [Narrator] After her
time in jail, Isabella

00:37:25.409 --> 00:37:27.787
was ordered to do community service,

00:37:27.787 --> 00:37:30.581
and also pay money, to
Caroline Criado-Perez,

00:37:30.581 --> 00:37:33.501
personally, for her online attacks.

00:37:35.586 --> 00:37:37.713
In England, Isabella Sorely's case,

00:37:37.713 --> 00:37:39.674
was a precedent setting one,

00:37:39.674 --> 00:37:42.760
a very clear message had
been sent by the crown.

00:37:42.760 --> 00:37:47.515
But not everyone agrees,
with the goverment's stance.

00:37:47.515 --> 00:37:50.434
- I think trolling is a free speech issue,

00:37:50.434 --> 00:37:52.103
I think what we have is a situation

00:37:52.103 --> 00:37:54.230
where people are being
prevented from saying

00:37:54.230 --> 00:37:56.399
certain things, often
quite shocking things,

00:37:56.399 --> 00:37:59.860
and not very socially
useful things, you know,

00:37:59.860 --> 00:38:03.072
it's often just insults
or abuse, but in my mind,

00:38:03.072 --> 00:38:05.950
it falls under the category
of freedom of speech.

00:38:05.950 --> 00:38:10.496
And if you aren't free to
hate, then you're not free.

00:38:10.496 --> 00:38:12.290
- [Narrator] Brandon O'
Neil is editor of the

00:38:12.290 --> 00:38:15.293
online current affairs magazine, Spiked.

00:38:15.293 --> 00:38:18.004
He's no stranger to controversy,

00:38:18.004 --> 00:38:19.338
or trolling.

00:38:19.338 --> 00:38:22.341
- Well, I've been attacked as
an individual all the time,

00:38:22.341 --> 00:38:24.802
I get abuse, I'm defamed, I'm libeled.

00:38:24.802 --> 00:38:26.470
If I search for myself online,

00:38:26.470 --> 00:38:28.055
there's all sorts of abuse.

00:38:28.055 --> 00:38:29.640
And sometimes it becomes physical.

00:38:29.640 --> 00:38:32.268
I've had an envelope of human shit

00:38:32.268 --> 00:38:34.186
sent to this office.

00:38:34.186 --> 00:38:36.647
So, you know, you get abuse all the time.

00:38:36.647 --> 00:38:38.399
If you put yourself out there,

00:38:38.399 --> 00:38:40.568
if you express an opinion,
if you say what you think,

00:38:40.568 --> 00:38:42.111
you will get abuse.

00:38:42.111 --> 00:38:44.238
But in my mind, that's the price you pay,

00:38:44.238 --> 00:38:46.407
for living in a free society

00:38:46.407 --> 00:38:50.578
in which everyone is allowed
to express their opinion.

00:38:51.704 --> 00:38:53.998
- [Narrator] Today,
opinions are everywhere,

00:38:53.998 --> 00:38:58.169
but increasingly, tolerance
of opposing views, is not.

00:38:59.295 --> 00:39:01.630
It seems, anyone can be
attacked for saying anything

00:39:01.630 --> 00:39:05.760
remotely offensive, and the
line between internet trolling,

00:39:05.760 --> 00:39:09.680
and what is called public
shaming, is blurring.

00:39:11.390 --> 00:39:13.100
- I guess, my definition,
that trolls are like a

00:39:13.100 --> 00:39:15.853
kind of extreme, outrageous
minority, who will use

00:39:15.853 --> 00:39:17.855
kind of extreme language.

00:39:17.855 --> 00:39:21.692
With the public shaming, it's
like nice people like us,

00:39:21.692 --> 00:39:25.363
we tear somebody apart because
we're trying to be good,

00:39:25.363 --> 00:39:27.073
kind, empathetic people.

00:39:27.073 --> 00:39:28.991
And we're the ones who
really do the damage,

00:39:28.991 --> 00:39:31.660
because being torn apart
by like tens of thousands

00:39:31.660 --> 00:39:33.245
of lovely people like us,

00:39:33.245 --> 00:39:35.581
for a joke that lands badly on Twitter,

00:39:35.581 --> 00:39:40.086
that is profoundly and
horrendously traumatizing.

00:39:40.086 --> 00:39:43.798
- [Narrator] Best selling
Welsh author, John Ronson,

00:39:43.798 --> 00:39:46.217
thinks that social media pile-ons,

00:39:46.217 --> 00:39:47.718
and the public humiliation

00:39:47.718 --> 00:39:49.804
that many of us take part in daily,

00:39:49.804 --> 00:39:51.305
is out of control.

00:39:52.556 --> 00:39:54.141
- Take somebody like Justine Sacco,

00:39:54.141 --> 00:39:57.186
you know, who just before
getting onto plane to Cape Town

00:39:57.186 --> 00:39:59.522
wrote, "Going to Africa,
hope I don't get AIDS.

00:39:59.522 --> 00:40:02.358
"Just kidding, I'm white."

00:40:02.358 --> 00:40:04.944
And she chuckled to
herself, and press send,

00:40:04.944 --> 00:40:06.529
and got no replies,

00:40:07.947 --> 00:40:11.951
got on the plane, turned
off her phone, fell asleep.

00:40:11.951 --> 00:40:16.664
Probably the last decent
sleep she had in a year.

00:40:16.664 --> 00:40:19.792
- [Narrator] Justine had
only 170 Twitter followers

00:40:19.792 --> 00:40:23.212
when she posted her now infamous joke.

00:40:23.212 --> 00:40:24.797
But as she slept on that flight,

00:40:24.797 --> 00:40:27.133
the internet exploded.

00:40:27.133 --> 00:40:29.802
When she woke up, and
turned her phone back on,

00:40:29.802 --> 00:40:32.721
she discovered that she had lost her job,

00:40:32.721 --> 00:40:35.933
and was now despised,
by a global audience,

00:40:35.933 --> 00:40:39.562
with millions of people
attacking her online.

00:40:39.562 --> 00:40:42.815
- Justine's tweet
overwhelmed your timeline,

00:40:42.815 --> 00:40:46.235
yet, everybody, me too, I
was in London, like in bed,

00:40:46.235 --> 00:40:48.195
and I thought what everbody else thought,

00:40:48.195 --> 00:40:51.615
which was, "Wow, somebody's fucked."

00:40:51.615 --> 00:40:54.243
(audience laughs)

00:40:54.243 --> 00:40:57.246
So, I sat up in bed, then I thought,

00:40:58.205 --> 00:40:59.582
"I'm not

00:40:59.582 --> 00:41:04.003
"certain that that was
intended to be a racist tweet."

00:41:04.003 --> 00:41:05.629
- [Narrator] John Ronson was so unnerved

00:41:05.629 --> 00:41:07.256
by the public response

00:41:07.256 --> 00:41:10.551
to Justine Sacco's tweet,
that he went to see her.

00:41:10.551 --> 00:41:12.803
- When I met Justine a
couple of weeks later,

00:41:12.803 --> 00:41:16.140
just crushed and broken, and mangled up,

00:41:17.099 --> 00:41:19.185
I asked her to explain
the joke, and she said,

00:41:19.185 --> 00:41:21.270
"Living in America, puts
us in a bit of a bubble

00:41:21.270 --> 00:41:23.105
"when it comes what's going
on in the third world.

00:41:23.105 --> 00:41:26.275
"I was making fun of the bubble."

00:41:26.275 --> 00:41:27.735
"And then came the trolls."

00:41:27.735 --> 00:41:31.572
"I'm actually kinda hoping
Justine Sacco gets AIDS, lol."

00:41:31.572 --> 00:41:32.740
Somebody else that night wrote,

00:41:32.740 --> 00:41:36.202
"Somebody HIV positive
should rape this bitch,

00:41:36.202 --> 00:41:38.496
"so, then we'll find it out
if her skin color protects her

00:41:38.496 --> 00:41:39.497
"from AIDS."

00:41:40.581 --> 00:41:43.501
But you know what, nobody
went after that person,

00:41:43.501 --> 00:41:44.627
that night.

00:41:44.627 --> 00:41:48.172
We were all so thrilled
to go after Justine,

00:41:48.172 --> 00:41:50.799
we were so happy to be destroying Justine,

00:41:50.799 --> 00:41:53.677
we didn't have enough space in our brains,

00:41:53.677 --> 00:41:56.096
to destroy somebody
who was inappropriately

00:41:56.096 --> 00:41:58.140
destroying Justine.

00:41:58.140 --> 00:42:00.726
So, that person got a free ride.

00:42:00.726 --> 00:42:02.144
With a public shaming,

00:42:02.144 --> 00:42:05.898
you are being told by hundreds
of thousands of lovely people

00:42:05.898 --> 00:42:07.483
that you are worthless.

00:42:07.483 --> 00:42:08.526
And the people who I met,

00:42:08.526 --> 00:42:12.238
who were really crushed,
traumatized, depressed,

00:42:12.238 --> 00:42:14.573
a smattering of trolls may have said,

00:42:14.573 --> 00:42:16.158
"We're gonna rape you, and kill you."

00:42:16.158 --> 00:42:18.327
But what really killed those people was,

00:42:18.327 --> 00:42:20.162
was our righteousness.

00:42:28.879 --> 00:42:31.340
- [Narrator] The line between
bad behavior and trolling,

00:42:31.340 --> 00:42:32.675
is blurry.

00:42:32.675 --> 00:42:36.679
And as Briton's most notorious
troll, Isabella Sorely,

00:42:36.679 --> 00:42:40.641
has found out, that
consequences are profound.

00:42:40.641 --> 00:42:43.394
She spent 12 weeks in jail for sending

00:42:43.394 --> 00:42:47.565
six threatening tweets, after
a night of binge drinking.

00:42:48.566 --> 00:42:51.151
- 20 minutes just still defining my life.

00:42:51.151 --> 00:42:54.655
Which, again, I struggle
to, to cope with that,

00:42:54.655 --> 00:42:57.074
'cause it was such a short period of time,

00:42:57.074 --> 00:43:00.077
and I'm always gonna have
that stigma attached,

00:43:00.077 --> 00:43:03.872
that I'm that Twitter, that Twitter girl.

00:43:03.872 --> 00:43:05.958
- [Narrator] She's sorry for what she did,

00:43:05.958 --> 00:43:10.254
but she's not sure the
punishment fit the crime.

00:43:10.254 --> 00:43:11.880
- I think, obviously what I sent,

00:43:11.880 --> 00:43:14.883
if you had said it face,
you're gonna get in trouble.

00:43:14.883 --> 00:43:18.554
I don't think I would've got prison.

00:43:18.554 --> 00:43:20.180
I was definitely made an example of,

00:43:20.180 --> 00:43:23.017
'cause of how high profile it was.

00:43:24.935 --> 00:43:26.478
- [Narrator] Isabella
would like to apologize,

00:43:26.478 --> 00:43:29.565
to her victim, face to face one day,

00:43:29.565 --> 00:43:31.567
but a lifetime restraining order means,

00:43:31.567 --> 00:43:34.111
that likely won't happen.

00:43:34.111 --> 00:43:36.739
Today, she works with youth
to educate them about the

00:43:36.739 --> 00:43:39.825
dangers of alcohol abuse,
which she believes,

00:43:39.825 --> 00:43:42.411
ignited her trolling behavior.

00:43:42.411 --> 00:43:45.831
But for now, and likely forever,
Isabella will have to live

00:43:45.831 --> 00:43:49.585
with the very public, and
criminal consequences,

00:43:49.585 --> 00:43:51.420
of her online actions.

00:44:01.847 --> 00:44:05.225
Free speech advocate and
journalist, Brandon O'Neil,

00:44:05.225 --> 00:44:06.435
doesn't mince words.

00:44:06.435 --> 00:44:10.564
He thinks Briton is
creeping closer to a 1984

00:44:10.564 --> 00:44:13.901
George Orwell police state,
when it comes to what

00:44:13.901 --> 00:44:16.612
can or cannot be said society.

00:44:16.612 --> 00:44:19.365
He sees trolls, as a scapegoated minority,

00:44:19.365 --> 00:44:22.993
pawns in a game where
much more is at stake.

00:44:24.078 --> 00:44:26.372
- We present the trolls as powerful,

00:44:26.372 --> 00:44:29.166
and the trolled, as these kind of victims,

00:44:29.166 --> 00:44:30.751
I think it's the other way around.

00:44:30.751 --> 00:44:34.546
I think troll hunters, the
people, usually the media people

00:44:34.546 --> 00:44:36.674
who present themselves
as these brave warriors

00:44:36.674 --> 00:44:38.676
against offensiveness on the internet,

00:44:38.676 --> 00:44:41.470
they are the greatest
threat to the internet.

00:44:41.470 --> 00:44:45.432
They are the ones undermining
the freedom of the internet,

00:44:45.432 --> 00:44:47.226
the accessibility of information,

00:44:47.226 --> 00:44:49.269
the right of people to express themselves.

00:44:49.269 --> 00:44:52.439
And it's the trolls, the sad man or woman,

00:44:52.439 --> 00:44:55.317
stuck in their bedroom, talking
rubbish on the internet,

00:44:55.317 --> 00:44:57.736
they are the victims.

00:44:57.736 --> 00:45:00.698
- [Narrator] As governments
legislate to deal with trolling,

00:45:00.698 --> 00:45:04.785
there maybe unintended
consequences for everyone,

00:45:04.785 --> 00:45:07.329
who communicates online.

00:45:07.329 --> 00:45:09.707
- And my concern, with
the way in which trolling

00:45:09.707 --> 00:45:12.626
is discussed is today, is that
everything gets conflated.

00:45:12.626 --> 00:45:15.129
So, you have a situation
where trolling can be

00:45:15.129 --> 00:45:17.589
everything from someone
issuing a death threat,

00:45:17.589 --> 00:45:19.591
which is obviously a serious thing to do,

00:45:19.591 --> 00:45:21.427
to someone writing an article saying,

00:45:21.427 --> 00:45:23.387
"I don't think climate
change is the biggest problem

00:45:23.387 --> 00:45:25.013
"facing humanity."

00:45:25.013 --> 00:45:27.057
Everything gets lumped
together as trolling,

00:45:27.057 --> 00:45:29.935
and if you believe in
freedom of speech as I do,

00:45:29.935 --> 00:45:32.980
you should be outraged by the arrest,

00:45:32.980 --> 00:45:36.483
not to mention the
imprisonment of someone simply

00:45:36.483 --> 00:45:38.402
for expressing an idea.

00:45:41.447 --> 00:45:42.489
- [Narrator] Six thousand kilometers

00:45:42.489 --> 00:45:44.575
away in Toronto, Canada,

00:45:44.575 --> 00:45:47.911
after three years of being
forced off the internet,

00:45:47.911 --> 00:45:50.789
having his artwork vandalized,

00:45:50.789 --> 00:45:53.834
and wondering he would go to jail for the

00:45:53.834 --> 00:45:55.419
tweets he sent,

00:45:55.419 --> 00:45:58.464
the verdict in Gregory
Allen Elliott's case,

00:45:58.464 --> 00:46:00.382
is finally handed down.

00:46:01.341 --> 00:46:04.386
He is innocent of all charges.

00:46:04.386 --> 00:46:06.346
The judge says his
tweet contained nothing,

00:46:06.346 --> 00:46:09.057
of a violent or sexual nature,

00:46:09.057 --> 00:46:12.144
and there was no indication
that he intended to hurt

00:46:12.144 --> 00:46:13.228
his accusers.

00:46:14.563 --> 00:46:16.064
- I was not doing anything wrong,

00:46:16.064 --> 00:46:19.777
and I was protecting myself
and my freedom of speech,

00:46:19.777 --> 00:46:23.030
and I wasn't gonna be bullied
or intimated by people

00:46:23.030 --> 00:46:25.574
that were trying to protect the world from

00:46:25.574 --> 00:46:27.159
bullies or intimidators, you know,

00:46:27.159 --> 00:46:30.162
for lack of a better word, you know.

00:46:31.288 --> 00:46:33.290
- [Narrator] Gregory has his freedom,

00:46:33.290 --> 00:46:36.084
but he's paid a heavy toll,
for the speech his opponents

00:46:36.084 --> 00:46:37.753
considered trolling.

00:46:39.213 --> 00:46:42.132
- The emotional toll's
brutal, it's brutal.

00:46:42.132 --> 00:46:44.718
And the monetary, the money and stuff,

00:46:44.718 --> 00:46:49.014
I lost my job of 17 years,
I had to cashout my pension.

00:46:49.014 --> 00:46:52.267
Absolutely destroyed me financially.

00:46:52.267 --> 00:46:54.353
Destroyed me financially.

00:46:55.354 --> 00:46:56.355
- [Narrator] Gregory's reputation,

00:46:56.355 --> 00:47:00.692
has also been damaged, even
his most abrasive tweets,

00:47:00.692 --> 00:47:03.278
pale beside the trolling he received,

00:47:03.278 --> 00:47:06.031
and the hands of the twitter mob.

00:47:06.031 --> 00:47:08.242
- Even though he was in the right,

00:47:08.242 --> 00:47:10.619
there is a permanent record
that's going to follow him

00:47:10.619 --> 00:47:13.580
on the internet, of Mr.
Elliott being labeled as a

00:47:13.580 --> 00:47:17.960
men's rights activist, a
misogynist, and a pedophile.

00:47:17.960 --> 00:47:22.714
And I guarantee you one
thing, nobody who accused him

00:47:22.714 --> 00:47:26.885
of being those three things,
is going to apologize.

00:47:28.637 --> 00:47:31.557
- [Narrator] Gregory's
detractors say that misogyny won,

00:47:31.557 --> 00:47:35.227
and the most vicious anti-feminist trolls,

00:47:35.227 --> 00:47:37.813
see his acquittal as a victory.

00:47:38.939 --> 00:47:41.275
Tragically, this case has done nothing,

00:47:41.275 --> 00:47:45.028
to make discourse on
the internet any better.

00:47:49.032 --> 00:47:53.287
Meanwhile, back in Chicago,
blogger Gina Crosley-Corocoran

00:47:53.287 --> 00:47:54.121
is tired.

00:47:55.581 --> 00:47:57.541
Despite the validation she gets from her

00:47:57.541 --> 00:47:59.835
Feminist Breeder Community,

00:47:59.835 --> 00:48:02.671
the trolls are wearing her down.

00:48:02.671 --> 00:48:05.883
Constant snarky comments, cruel pranks,

00:48:05.883 --> 00:48:07.676
threats against her family,

00:48:07.676 --> 00:48:09.928
and late night police intrusions,

00:48:09.928 --> 00:48:13.932
she's learned to manage
it, but it's exhausting.

00:48:15.809 --> 00:48:17.269
- I wish that people would
use the internet more as a

00:48:17.269 --> 00:48:20.564
buffet, just take what you
want and leave the rest,

00:48:20.564 --> 00:48:22.941
as opposed to feeling like
anything that doesn't apply

00:48:22.941 --> 00:48:25.027
to them, or anything
that they're bothered by,

00:48:25.027 --> 00:48:27.738
is a reason to go on
the offensive, you know.

00:48:27.738 --> 00:48:29.823
When I go to the buffet,

00:48:31.575 --> 00:48:33.410
I like the chicken, and I
like the mac and cheese,

00:48:33.410 --> 00:48:36.371
but I'm not insulted by the
other things that are there,

00:48:36.371 --> 00:48:38.373
that are might be useful for other people,

00:48:38.373 --> 00:48:39.875
and other people might find paletable.

00:48:39.875 --> 00:48:42.085
I'm just like, "Yeah,
those aren't for me."

00:48:42.085 --> 00:48:44.504
And I walk away, I don't
like, go up to the manager,

00:48:44.504 --> 00:48:45.339
and scream,

00:48:45.339 --> 00:48:46.840
"How dare you have this
available at your buffet.

00:48:46.840 --> 00:48:48.550
"It doesn't personally meet my needs."

00:48:48.550 --> 00:48:50.427
But I feel like a lot of
people on the internet,

00:48:50.427 --> 00:48:53.764
that that's exactly the
way that they react.

00:48:53.764 --> 00:48:55.849
- [Narrator] Will this new
psychological phenomenon

00:48:55.849 --> 00:48:58.352
of trolling ever end?

00:48:58.352 --> 00:49:00.479
The answer is likely, no.

00:49:00.479 --> 00:49:02.773
Instead, we need to gain the tools,

00:49:02.773 --> 00:49:05.025
to recognize how to fight trolling,

00:49:05.025 --> 00:49:09.112
and recognize how not to
become trolls ourselves.

00:49:10.322 --> 00:49:13.492
Perhaps ironically,
notorious troll, Wild Goose,

00:49:13.492 --> 00:49:16.703
advocates for anonymity,
and cautions that our real,

00:49:16.703 --> 00:49:20.123
physical lives, should
be kept off the internet,

00:49:20.123 --> 00:49:21.249
altogether.

00:49:21.249 --> 00:49:24.586
- People need to disassociate themselves

00:49:26.880 --> 00:49:29.216
from their internet persona.

00:49:30.133 --> 00:49:32.928
I think it's dangerous, to

00:49:32.928 --> 00:49:36.515
sort of plug your personality
into the internet.

00:49:36.515 --> 00:49:39.476
You're leaving yourself open
to a lot of dangerous actors,

00:49:39.476 --> 00:49:43.855
more dangerous people than
the Bill Waggoner Crew.

00:49:43.855 --> 00:49:46.900
Privacy is just as big of an issue,

00:49:46.900 --> 00:49:50.487
as freedom of speech,
they go hand in hand.

00:49:52.698 --> 00:49:55.575
- [Narrator] We think of our
real world, physical lives,

00:49:55.575 --> 00:49:59.204
as distinct from our online activity,

00:49:59.204 --> 00:50:02.708
but that separation is
rapidly breaking down.

00:50:02.708 --> 00:50:04.751
The biggest challenge we face,

00:50:04.751 --> 00:50:06.545
as we adapt to this change,

00:50:06.545 --> 00:50:10.757
is maintaining our freedoms
in the digital space.

00:50:10.757 --> 00:50:15.512
Freedom of speech, maybe the
most difficult to transition.

00:50:15.512 --> 00:50:17.931
For first time in human history,

00:50:17.931 --> 00:50:20.642
the masses can express their opinions,

00:50:20.642 --> 00:50:22.477
in a published forum.

00:50:22.477 --> 00:50:23.770
I think that's a wonderful thing,

00:50:23.770 --> 00:50:25.939
and my concern with troll hunting,

00:50:25.939 --> 00:50:30.360
is that it's undermining that
demographic breakthrough.

00:50:30.360 --> 00:50:32.738
- [Narrator] Physical threats
should never be tolerated.

00:50:32.738 --> 00:50:36.158
But we may need to ensure
that others have the right

00:50:36.158 --> 00:50:40.328
to disagree with us, insult,
even hate us, online.

00:50:42.205 --> 00:50:44.166
- If we are serious
about freedom of speech,

00:50:44.166 --> 00:50:46.835
then we have to allow
people to say, hateful,

00:50:46.835 --> 00:50:50.213
obnoxious, racist, sexist, things.

00:50:50.213 --> 00:50:52.424
Freedom of speech covers
all forms of speech.

00:50:52.424 --> 00:50:55.177
(dramatic music)

00:51:12.235 --> 00:51:14.946
(dramatic music)

